<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Plan 53 §3.4's W1 — THE USER'S OWN SCENARIO — under mode `takes-split`
  (QBX-172). Driven through `place-recording mode="takes-split"` directly, so
  the whole case is arithmetic.

  W1 is TWO ADJACENT CHILDREN and one pass that covers the first entirely and
  the second only partway. §3.4's mode-B bullet: "independent per child, left
  to right; the recording is windowed to each piece it covers", giving
  `{A/R}`, `{B1/R'}` and a plain `B2`.

  GEOMETRY. test_sawtooth.wav is 192000 frames (4 s at 48 kHz):

      A = [0,     48000)      the first child
      B = [48000, 144000)     the second, abutting it exactly
      R = [0,     96000)      the pass, covering A whole and B halfway

  and the expected result is

      child 0   [0,     48000)   A, now a column: take 0 = A, take 1 = R
      child 1   [48000, 96000)   B's head, a column: take 0 = B1, take 1 = R'
      child 2   [96000, 144000)  B's tail, a plain clip, srcOffset 48000
      child 3   does not exist

  THE TAKE ANCHORS ARE THE WHOLE POINT, and they are what no other case in
  this set checks. "The recording is windowed to each piece it covers" means
  each piece gets the part of the pass that lies over IT, so

      child 0's take 1   srcOffset 0       (the pass from its own start)
      child 1's take 1   srcOffset 48000   (the pass from 48000 frames in)

  A planner that handed the whole pass to every piece would give both takes
  srcOffset 0, and every structural assertion above would still pass: two
  columns, the right edges, the right take counts. So `take="1" startOffset=`
  on each column is the assertion that separates a correct implementation from
  a plausible one, and the reason `assert-clip-window`'s `take=` attribute
  exists at all.

  WHY IT FAILS ON main: `takes-split` REFUSES there (QBX-171 shipped it as
  `NotYetImplemented`, loud on purpose rather than falling back), so
  `place-recording` is rejected and every assertion after it fails. It would
  also fail against a silent fallback to `takes`: `LegacyTakes` would make A a
  take column and then SKIP B entirely, because B starts at 48000 which is
  below the cursor A left behind, so child 1 would be B unchanged at
  [48000,144000) and child 2 would not exist.

  NOTHING IS SNAPPED HERE. The children abut exactly and the pass starts on
  A's own start, so §3.5's sliver rule would have nothing to move even if it
  ran — and it does not, because this case drives the verb and never the
  recorder.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="rec_split_w1">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <snap-to-grid-disable/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <!-- A and B, abutting at 48000. Different source offsets so a mix-up
         between the two children is visible in the windows below. -->
    <place-clip trackPath="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav"
                timePos="0" startOffset="0" duration="48000"/>
    <place-clip trackPath="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav"
                timePos="48000" startOffset="0" duration="96000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="48000"
                        startOffset="0"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" startTime="48000" duration="96000"
                        startOffset="0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="none"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,1" shape="none"/>

    <!-- The pass: A whole, B halfway. -->
    <place-recording trackPath="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav"
                     mode="takes-split" timePos="0" length="96000"/>

    <!-- A became a column, at its own extent. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="48000"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="1"
                        comp=""/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" take="1" startOffset="0"/>

    <!-- B's head became its own column, and its take carries the pass from
         48000 frames in. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" startTime="48000" duration="48000"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,1" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="1"
                        comp=""/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" take="1" startOffset="48000"/>
    <!-- AND THE ANCHOR ASSERTION IS NOT VACUOUS. `assert-clip-window`'s
         `take=` is silently ignored on the WRAPPED shape, so a case that only
         ever asserts the right value cannot tell "read take 1 and it matched"
         from "ignored the attribute and read the column". Asserting the WRONG
         anchor and requiring a REJECTION is what pins it: 0 is what a planner
         handing the whole pass to every piece would leave here, which is the
         mistake this case exists to catch. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" take="1" startOffset="0"
                        expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- B's tail is untouched material, and its own source anchor advanced
         with the cut. -->
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,2" startTime="96000" duration="48000"
                        startOffset="48000"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,2" shape="none"/>

    <assert-clip-window clip="0,3" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- One composite, one undo. -->
    <undo count="1"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="48000"
                        startOffset="0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="none"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" startTime="48000" duration="96000"
                        startOffset="0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,1" shape="none"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,2" expectReject="true"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
